
<p>The <a href="https://daringfireball.net/2025/03/a_postscript_on_the_singular_nature_of_mark_gurmans_reporting">incomparable</a> Mark Gurman, reporting for Bloomberg:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Apple Inc.’s upcoming push into smart home devices will include a
doorbell, thermostat and other accessories developed through an
unusual partnership with LG Electronics Inc.</p>

<p>The products will be part of an ecosystem of devices that work
with Apple’s new smart home hub, as well as an upgraded HomePod
mini and fresh TV set-top box, according to people with knowledge
of the matter. The new Apple devices are set to be introduced on
Oct. 13.</p>

<p>The products developed with South Korea’s LG will also include a
smart deadbolt lock, indoor security camera, outdoor security
camera and floodlight camera, said the people, who asked not to be
identified because the undertaking is private.</p>

<p>Though the two companies worked together to develop the
accessories, the products will carry the LG brand. The South
Korean firm is handling manufacturing and product support.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Gurman has more than “people familiar with the matter” (or Matter, for that matter) — he cites FCC filings from LG that have product names and illustrations of some of the devices’ rear panels (with the connectors).</p>

<p>As Gurman himself notes, this is somewhat unusual for Apple, but it’s not altogether new territory either. In Venn diagram terms, the circle for “peripherals Apple very much wants on the market” is a superset of “peripherals Apple wants to make itself and put the Apple brand on”. For some of these products that Apple doesn’t want to make under its own brand, Apple makes them through Beats. But for others they partner with other companies. They’ve long <a href="https://www.apple.com/us/search/logitech">partnered with Logitech</a> for keyboards, keyboard iPad cases, and even the <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/product/hq6q2vc/a/logitech-crayon-for-ipad">Logitech Crayon</a>, the only third-party alternative to Apple’s own official iPad Pencils. Unlike Apple’s Pencils, the Crayon works without pairing to a specific iPad — surely a godsend in classrooms. There’s also the <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/product/hs9u2zm/a/logitech-muse">Logitech Muse</a> — an air-pencil/magic-wand peripheral for Vision Pro. (Vision Pro is a $4000 mixed reality headset that weighs 2 pounds, which Apple released in early 2024.) <a href="https://www.apple.com/us/search/belkin">Apple does this with Belkin too</a> — that’s another trusted partner for Apple.</p>

<p>And, in addition to serving as a longtime display component supplier for Apple, back when Apple had temporarily and foolishly exited the market for Apple-branded external displays, they worked with LG to make the LG 5K Ultrafine display, which for a while was simultaneously the best external Mac display on the market yet <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2017/01/31/hall-lg-5k">also</a> an <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2020/01/22/catalina-lg-5k-brightness">unreliable</a> <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2017/02/03/lg-fix">flaky</a> product. Even at its best it had an unstable sub-par stand.</p>

<p>The thing that makes this partnership awkward for Apple is that these products — doorbells, cameras, deadbolt locks — are products that require the utmost of trust. What products could possibly require more trust than a camera with footage of what’s going on in or at your home, or a lock that controls who can get in? I think that’s why Apple wants to get into this market with products that they can put their stamp of approval on. But LG, of all companies, <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/991190/lg-tv-spying-standby-recording-wi-fi-scanning-gamers-nexus">was just exposed in a scandal</a> for shipping “smart” TVs that literally listen to the ambient audio in the room — even in standby! — and invade your privacy by tracking what content appears on screen. (I own a 2019 LG TV but I haven’t allowed it Wi-Fi access since, literally, 2019.)</p>

